# How a language could do this

Firstly, we're going to assume that Rust has a stable ABI, and a way to ask the REPL information about a specified function.

I've implemented something like this (not via the REPL though), we'll talk about that in part 3.





When you're a solo [BDFL](https://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=BDFL) language developer leading a rag-tag team of volunteers, your time is precious.

But their time is precious too.


I've worked on Vale for a long time. I've been through the flaming trenches of seven different iterations of generics, [# "Generics" is a common swear word in my discord server.] I've repelled the incursion of Windows bugs in five different campaigns, [# Huge thanks to Jon Goodwin for showing me the path towards initial Windows support, way back in the beginning!] and I've done three LLVM migrations. I've been through hell and back. *In other words, I'm committed.*

But most of my volunteers are just interested in playing with a new language, and helping out a bit. They like working on problems that are challenging in interesting ways. They're coding for fun, and to help out the project a little bit.


When you lead engineers at your company, your main job is to *get the obstacles out of their way* so they can make something great. You're often [running interference](https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/run+interference), interacting with managers and other teams and getting sign-offs so that your engineers can focus on building an actual solution.

When leading volunteers, you also need to make the project challenging in a good way, and not challenging in the bad ways. This usually means working with them, 
